# 100% Line and Branch Coverage Rule for All Tests

This rule defines the requirements and best practices for achieving 100% line and branch coverage in any kind of test, including unit, integration, and system tests.

## Coverage Requirements
- Every line of code must be executed by at least one test (100% line coverage)
- Every branch of every conditional (if, else, switch, ternary, etc.) must be executed in all possible directions (100% branch coverage)
- All exception and error branches must be tested
- All edge cases, including null, empty, and boundary values, must be covered

## Best Practices
- Write tests for both positive and negative scenarios
- Test all logical paths, including true/false for all conditions
- Use parameterized tests to cover multiple input combinations
- Use mocks and stubs to simulate dependencies and edge cases
- Test exception handling and error propagation
- Use code coverage tools (e.g., JaCoCo, Istanbul, Coverage.py) to verify coverage
- Refactor code to make it more testable if necessary

## Example Scenarios
- All branches of if/else, switch, and ternary operators
- All catch and finally blocks
- All early returns and guard clauses
- All input validation and error handling logic
- All combinations of boolean flags and configuration options
- All public API endpoints and service methods

## How to Use
- Run your test suite with coverage enabled
- Review the coverage report and identify any uncovered lines or branches
- Add or update tests to cover all missing lines and branches
- Repeat until 100% line and branch coverage is achieved

This rule applies to all test types and all languages. Adapt the examples and practices to your specific codebase and technology stack.
